// from the class-30 notes on the parking lot, written BEFORE the vending machine was built:
// - TicketManager.getAllTicketHistory() leaks the internal mutable list

Compare the note written during the parking lot retrospective with the vending machine class written afterwards. What does this tell you about the value of that retrospective as it was used?,"The note was wrong, which is why it was ignored","Writing a problem down does not prevent repeating it - the identical leak was reproduced in the next project, because nothing turned the note into something enforced like a review checklist or a test",The vending machine version is safe because it uses ArrayList rather than LinkedList,The two situations are unrelated,,2,"The most useful thing about spotting this in the quiz is the pattern, not the individual bug: retrospective notes that stay as prose tend to be re-learned rather than applied.",1

Real code:

// VendingMachine
public List<Slot> getSlots() {
    return slots;
}

// InventoryReportService
public int getTotalStock(VendingMachine machine) {
    int total = 0;
    for (Slot slot : machine.getSlots()) {
        total += slot.getQuantity();
    }
    return total;
}

The reporting service reads the machine's slots through this getter. Beyond reading them, what else does this getter currently permit, and does the reporting service need that?,Nothing else - a getter can only be used to read,"It permits any caller to add or remove slots from the machine's live inventory list, which the reporting service never needs - it only iterates","It permits changing the price of a slot, which reporting does need","It permits nothing extra, since Slot has no setters",,2,"This is the same leak as the transaction history, in the class that was supposed to be the safe read-only consumer. A read-only view would give the reporting service everything it uses and nothing it does not.",2

Real code:

// ChangeService
public boolean canMakeChange(double amount) {
    return changeReserve >= amount;
}

public void releaseChange(double amount) {
    if (amount > changeReserve) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("not enough change reserve to release " + amount);
    }
    changeReserve -= amount;
}

// facade, earlier in completePurchase()
if (!machine.canMakeChange(change)) { ... throw new ChangeUnavailableException(...); }

The facade has already checked canMakeChange() before releaseChange() is ever reached, so the throw inside releaseChange() can never fire in the current flow. Should it be deleted as dead code?,Yes - unreachable code should always be removed,"No. It protects ChangeService from any future caller who forgets to check first, and it states the class's own rule rather than depending on a promise made in a different class","Yes - the facade check makes it impossible to reach, so it is misleading","No, because Java requires a throw in any method that modifies a field",,2,"Two different jobs: the facade's check produces a clean customer-facing outcome, while this one stops the reserve going negative no matter who calls it. Only the second one still holds if someone writes a new caller.",2

Real code:

public class AwaitingPaymentState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("a selection is already in progress, cancel first");
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        machine.getPendingPurchase().addAmountReceived(amount);
        System.out.println("received " + amount + ", total so far: "
                + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("cancelling, refunding " + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
    }
}

A customer selects the 1.75 Soda, inserts a 1.00 coin, then presses cancel. Follow the code: the message prints 'cancelling, refunding 1.0'. What happens to the actual coin?,It is returned via ChangeService.releaseChange(1.00),Nothing happens to it. No line in cancel() touches the change reserve or returns anything - the machine physically holds a coin that no part of the system now records,It is added to the reserve as machine income,It is credited to the next customer's purchase,,2,"The class even carries a comment admitting this. It is the same defect shape as the parking lot's release(): output that reads like an action, with no action behind it.",2

