private Transaction completePurchase(PaymentMethod method) {
    Slot slot = machine.getPendingPurchase().getSlot();
    double amountReceived = machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived();
    double change = amountReceived - slot.getPrice();

    if (!machine.canMakeChange(change)) {
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
        throw new ChangeUnavailableException("cannot make change of " + change
                + ", refunding " + amountReceived);
    }

    machine.setState(DispensingState.getInstance());

    slot.dispenseOne();

    IPaymentStrategy strategy = paymentStrategyFactory.getStrategy(method);

    if (strategy.doesAffectChangeReserve()) {
        machine.releaseChange(change);
        machine.receiveCash(amountReceived);
    }

    Transaction transaction = machine.getPendingPurchase()
            .complete(change, method, System.currentTimeMillis());
    transactionManager.save(transaction);

    machine.resetTransaction();
    machine.setState(machine.hasAnyStock() ? IdleState.getInstance() : UnavailableState.getInstance());

    return transaction;
}

Same 6.00 item, reversed order: 5.00 by card, then 1.00 in cash. Measured: the reserve reads 50.0 before and 56.0 after, a rise of 6.00, although only one 1.00 coin was inserted. Which line is responsible?,"slot.dispenseOne(), which miscounts the sale","machine.receiveCash(amountReceived) - it is passed the whole purchase total rather than just the cash portion, so 5.00 of card money is recorded as coins that are not in the machine","machine.releaseChange(change), which adds instead of subtracting","The Transaction constructor, which stores the wrong amount",,2,Together with the previous question this shows one root cause producing opposite symptoms: money vanishing in one ordering and appearing from nowhere in the other. Both come from amountReceived being treated as if a single method paid all of it.,2

Real code:

public class UPIPayment implements IPaymentStrategy {

    @Override
    public boolean doesAffectChangeReserve() {
        return true;
    }
}

// VendingMachine.java

// The cash in the vending machine should not update if you are paying via UPI or card,
// because then the cash is going into the bank account of the merchant
// and not into the amount reservoir of the vending machine.

public void receiveCash(double amount) {
    changeService.receiveCash(amount);
}

Read the override against the rule stated in the comment. What does a UPI purchase currently do to the machine's coin reserve, and should it?,"It leaves the reserve untouched, which is correct",It updates the reserve - adding the purchase total as if coins had been inserted and paying change out of the coin box - which directly contradicts the stated rule that UPI money goes to the merchant's bank,"It only pays out change and never adds, which is correct for UPI","It throws an exception, so the situation never arises",,2,"CashPayment should be the only type returning true. Correcting UPIPayment to inherit the false default is a one-line fix, and the fact that the code disagrees with a comment sitting a few files away is what made it easy to miss.",2

Real code:

public class IdleState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        Slot slot = machine.findSlot(slotId);
        if (slot == null || !slot.hasStock()) {
            throw new SlotUnavailableException("slot " + slotId + " is not available");
        }
        machine.startPurchase(slot);
        machine.setState(AwaitingPaymentState.getInstance());
        System.out.println("selected " + slot.getProductName() + ", price: " + slot.getPrice());
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("select a product before inserting payment");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is idle");
    }
}

// Slot
public void dispenseOne() {
    if (quantity <= 0) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("slot " + id + " is out of stock");
    }
    quantity--;
}

IdleState.selectSlot() already refuses a slot with no stock, so by the time dispenseOne() runs the slot was known to have stock. Is the check inside dispenseOne() therefore pointless?,Yes - it duplicates a check that has already passed,"No. Time and several steps pass between selection and dispensing, and dispenseOne() is a public method any future code could call directly - the guard keeps the quantity from going negative regardless of who calls it or when",Yes - the state classes are the correct place for all validation,"No, because Java requires bounds checks on integer decrements",,2,A class should defend its own invariant rather than trust that every caller checked first. The selection-time check is about giving the customer a good message

