private Transaction completePurchase(PaymentMethod method) {
    Slot slot = machine.getPendingPurchase().getSlot();
    double amountReceived = machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived();
    double change = amountReceived - slot.getPrice();

    if (!machine.canMakeChange(change)) {
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
        throw new ChangeUnavailableException("cannot make change of " + change
                + ", refunding " + amountReceived);
    }

    machine.setState(DispensingState.getInstance());

    slot.dispenseOne();

    IPaymentStrategy strategy = paymentStrategyFactory.getStrategy(method);

    if (strategy.doesAffectChangeReserve()) {
        machine.releaseChange(change);
        machine.receiveCash(amountReceived);
    }

    Transaction transaction = machine.getPendingPurchase()
            .complete(change, method, System.currentTimeMillis());
    transactionManager.save(transaction);

    machine.resetTransaction();
    machine.setState(machine.hasAnyStock() ? IdleState.getInstance() : UnavailableState.getInstance());

    return transaction;
}

public class UnavailableState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("machine is unavailable (out of stock)");
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("machine is unavailable (out of stock)");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is unavailable");
    }
}

This ternary on the last line is the only place in the entire project that puts the machine into UnavailableState, and no code anywhere calls Slot.restock(). What is the consequence once the final item is sold?,The machine returns to IdleState on the next attempted selection,"The machine is stuck permanently: every operation in UnavailableState either throws or does nothing, and there is no trigger anywhere that moves it back to Idle - even refilling a slot would not help",The machine automatically restocks itself after a delay,completePurchase() would throw on the next purchase attempt,,2,A genuine hole rather than a subtle bug: the design has a way in and no way out. Any real machine needs a technician-facing restock operation that both refills slots and returns the machine to Idle.,2

Real code:

// Transaction stores exactly one method per purchase
public Transaction(String slotId, String productName, double price, double changeGiven,
                   PaymentMethod paymentMethod, long timestamp) { ... }

public Transaction insertPayment(double amount, PaymentMethod method) {
    IPaymentStrategy strategy = paymentStrategyFactory.getStrategy(method);

    double credited = strategy.process(amount);

    machine.insertPayment(credited);

    Slot slot = machine.getPendingPurchase().getSlot();
    if (machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived() >= slot.getPrice()) {
        return completePurchase(method);
    }
    return null;
}

Because a strategy is selected per insertPayment() call, one purchase can be paid partly in cash and partly by card. What does the saved Transaction record in that case?,"Both methods, stored as a list",Only the method of the call that happened to complete the purchase - every earlier method used for the same purchase is lost from the record,The method used for the largest portion of the payment,CASH always takes priority in the record,,2,"The Transaction was designed as though exactly one method pays for the whole purchase, while the payment flow allows several. Auditing a mixed purchase from the saved records would be impossible.",2

Real code:

public Transaction insertPayment(double amount, PaymentMethod method) {
    IPaymentStrategy strategy = paymentStrategyFactory.getStrategy(method);

    double credited = strategy.process(amount);

    machine.insertPayment(credited);

    Slot slot = machine.getPendingPurchase().getSlot();
    if (machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived() >= slot.getPrice()) {
        return completePurchase(method);
    }
    return null;
}

A customer selects the 2.50 Chips and inserts 100.00 in cash. The machine holds only 5.00 in change. Trace what the customer experiences.,The purchase completes and the machine gives 97.50 in change it does not have,"canMakeChange(97.50) fails, so ChangeUnavailableException is thrown and nothing is dispensed - but note that the exception message says 'refunding 100.0' while no code actually returns the money",The machine dispenses the Chips and records a debt of 97.50,insertPayment() returns null and the machine waits for more money,,2,The refusal itself is correct and happens before anything is dispensed. The gap is the same one cancel() has: the message promises a refund that no line of code performs.,2

Real code:

private Transaction completePurchase(PaymentMethod method) {
    Slot slot = machine.getPendingPurchase().getSlot();
    double amountReceived = machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived();
    double change = amountReceived - slot.getPrice();

    if (!machine.canMakeChange(change)) {
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
        throw new ChangeUnavailableException("cannot make change of " + change
                + ", refunding " + amountReceived);
    }

    machine.setState(DispensingState.getInstance());

    slot.dispenseOne();

    IPaymentStrategy strategy = paymentStrategyFactory.getStrategy(method);

    if (strategy.doesAffectChangeReserve()) {
        machine.releaseChange(change);
        machine.receiveCash(amountReceived);
    }

    Transaction transaction = machine.getPendingPurchase()
            .complete(change, method, System.currentTimeMillis());
    transactionManager.save(transaction);

    machine.resetTransaction();
    machine.setState(machine.hasAnyStock() ? IdleState.getInstance() : UnavailableState.getInstance());

    return transaction;
}

Looking at the whole method, the change amount is calculated once as 'amountReceived - slot.getPrice()' and then used in two places: the canMakeChange() check and, later, releaseChange(). What property of this method makes that reuse safe?,Nothing makes it safe - it should be recalculated before releaseChange(),"Nothing between the two uses can alter amountReceived or the slot price: the pending purchase is not modified until resetTransaction() at the very end, so the value cannot have gone stale",It is safe because change is declared final,It is safe because double values are immutable in Java,,2,"Worth having students actually check rather than assume. The reasoning is about what the code between the two uses does - and here, nothing between them touches either input.",2
